<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01Z0">Remark <span data-tag="01Z0">3.1.6.5</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of Kan complexes. Then $f$ is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ if and only if it is a <em>homotopy equivalence</em>: that is, there exists a morphism of Kan complexes $g: Y \rightarrow X$ such that $f \circ g$ and $g \circ f$ are homotopic to the identity maps $\operatorname{id}_{Y}$ and $\operatorname{id}_{X}$, respectively (see §<a data-tag="00U1" href="/tag/00U1">3.1.7</a>). </p>
</article>
